Here `count` is inferred as `int`. `count = 'four'` would be an error because the type is fixed as int after inference.

```dart
String? input = readInput();
// int n = input.length;          // error: input may be null
int n = input?.length ?? 0;       // safe
if (input != null) {
  print(input.length);            // promoted to String
}
```
After the null check, Dart promotes `input` to non-nullable inside the if block (for local variables).

